NOTE Segment 1 — Hook (0:00-0:15, G solo)

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
<v G>You're watching a video about a portfolio.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.500
<v G>The portfolio is at hrekov-dot-dev.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.500
<v G>My voice, my face, this whole edit —
every second of it was generated by AI.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.000
<v G>That's not the trick.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.000
<v G>The trick is what's on the other tab.

NOTE Segment 2 — Q1: problem + memory folder (0:15-1:30, H↔G)

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.000
<v H>Every large language model, in every session, starts from zero.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.500
<v H>It forgets everything.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.000
<v H>How do you actually solve that in a project
that ships across, what, twelve sprints?

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.500
<v G>Seventeen markdown files in a folder.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.500
<v G>That's the whole thing.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:36.000
<v G>Not a vector store, not a database —
just files, with frontmatter, one file per idea.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.000
<v G>Organized by what it's for.
User, feedback, project, reference.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.500
<v G>What's on screen right now is one of them.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.500
<v G>It's a rule about the CSS cascade,
born from one wasted hour debugging Tailwind on an anchor tag.

NOTE 2-sec pause here per script §2

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.500
<v G>That hour paid for itself the next session,
and every session after.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.500
<v G>And unlike a vector store, I can read it.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.000
<v G>I can delete a line I disagree with.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.500
<v G>I can grep it.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:11.000
<v G>The persistence layer is legible on purpose —
because the person maintaining it is a human, not a model.

NOTE Segment 3 — Q2: index file (1:30-2:05, H↔G)

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.500
<v H>So does Claude read all seventeen files
at the start of every session?

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.500
<v H>That sounds expensive.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:40.500
<v G>No. It reads one — an index file.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:46.500
<v G>The index has a one-line hook per memory,
so Claude knows the shape of every file without opening it.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:52.500
<v G>When something in the current turn matches a hook,
Claude pulls that file in.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.000
<v G>First-turn context is small; recall is on-demand.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.500
<v G>The pattern's not new.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:03.000
<v G>Nobody talks about applying it to memory.

NOTE Segment 4 — Q3: classifier (2:05-2:55, H↔G)

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.500
<v H>You mentioned a classifier earlier.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.000
<v H>Nine classes for every message you send.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.500
<v H>That sounds like the kind of ceremony
that makes people abandon the discipline after week two.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.500
<v H>Why isn't it just overhead?

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.500
<v G>It's the opposite.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.500
<v G>Every message gets classified before Claude responds.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:31.500
<v G>Question, bug, small feature, large feature,
refactor, testing, discovery, memory, observer.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.500
<v G>The class picks the model —

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.000
<v G>Haiku for fast, Sonnet for middle,
Opus for the ones that shape architecture.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.000
<v G>The class also picks the ceremony.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.500
<v G>A bug gets diagnosed, fixed, logged.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.000
<v G>A large feature gets a written spec
before a single line of code is allowed.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.000
<v G>Nothing gets built by accident.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.500
<v G>When people say AI is unreliable,
what they usually mean is they let the AI decide what kind of turn it was.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.500
<v G>That's the unreliability. Not the model.

NOTE Segment 5 — Q4: the proof (2:55-3:40, H↔G)
NOTE Cue times below start at 3:00 to avoid overlap with §4 tail —
NOTE final timing in Phase E5 will realign to actual audio boundaries.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.000
<v H>Okay, prove it.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.000
<v H>Every AI-workflow demo I've seen makes big claims
and shows you a screenshot from three weeks ago.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:15.000
<v H>Show me the numbers you just quoted
actually match what's on disk right now —

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.500
<v H>not what you wrote in a doc back when it was true.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.500
<v G>Twelve sprints in eight calendar days.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.500
<v G>Twenty-five journal entries.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:31.000
<v G>Nine of them graduated into a rules file
that Claude reads before anything else —

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
<v G>that's a thirty-six-percent promotion rate,
and it's the number I actually watch.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.500
<v G>High means we're generalizing.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.500
<v G>Low means we're solving the same problem twice.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:47.000
<v G>And a script runs at build time
to make sure every number I just said matches what's on disk.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.000
<v G>So this video can't quietly rot either.

